course: dictionary
lesson: 8302
title: Short words and abbreviations
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An abbreviation is a short way of writing a word or phrase. Writers use them to save time and space. Some abbreviations are the same letters but mean different things, so the context tells you which meaning is right.
explain: In a text message, «I'm busy rn» uses rn to mean right now. On a hospital badge, «Jane Smith, RN» means she is a Registered Nurse. In a story about ships, «HMS Victory, RN» points to the Royal Navy. Look at the words around the letters to decide.
explain: Texting is quick and informal, so rn is fine there. In school writing, you should spell the words out. The message «bro come here rn» becomes «Come here right now.» in your schoolwork.
fact: An abbreviation is a short form of a word or phrase.
fact: «rn» means right now, and it is slang used in texts and online chats.
fact: «RN» can mean Registered Nurse, a fully trained nurse in the USA, Canada and Australia.
fact: «RN» can also mean the Royal Navy, the navy of the United Kingdom.
fact: Use the context to decide which meaning is right, and write words in full in your school work.
exercise: «I'm busy rn.» What does rn mean here? | Really nice | Royal Navy | *right now | Registered Nurse
exercise: A hospital badge says «Jane Smith, RN». What does RN mean? | Really new | *Registered Nurse | Royal Navy | right now
exercise: Ryan texts «bro come here rn». How should you write this in school writing? | *Come here right now. | Come here rn!! | bro come here rn | bro, come here RN
test: In a story, «HMS Ariel, RN» sails the sea. What does RN mean? | Really nice | Right now | *Royal Navy | Registered Nurse
test: A friend types «see you rn». What does rn mean? | Really new | *right now | Registered Nurse | Royal Navy
exam: Mia sees «RN» on a nurse's badge and «rn» in a text. Why do they mean different things? | *The context changes the meaning, and they can be different abbreviations | They are always spelled with capital letters, so they mean the same | Texts use only the Royal Navy meaning | Nurses never use abbreviations
